course: dictionary
lesson: 15057
title: Reverberating: sounds that keep coming back
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words describe a sound that does not stop at once. «The drum gave a reverberating boom that filled the whole hall.» Look at the clues: a boom that fills the hall is loud, and it lasts. So reverberating means echoing again and again, filling the space.
explain: The same word can also describe effects. «The news of the discovery is still reverberating around the world.» News cannot echo like a sound, so the word is used in a new way: the news keeps spreading and having effects for a long time. Ask yourself: is this about a sound, or about something spreading? The sentence will tell you.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Her shout kept reverberating around the empty cave.» If you put in echoing, the sentence still makes sense: «Her shout kept echoing around the empty cave.» That is a good sign your meaning is right.
fact: Reverberating is the -ing form of the verb reverberate.
fact: «Reverberating» means echoing again and again, loudly, filling a space.
fact: In science, sound bounces off hard walls in halls, caves and tunnels, so it can reverberate.
fact: «Reverberating» can also mean having effects that go on spreading for a long time, like news.
fact: Soft things such as carpets and curtains soak up sound, so a room full of them does not reverberate much.
exercise: «Her shout kept reverberating around the empty cave.» What is reverberating? | Whispering softly | Fading away quickly | *Echoing again and again | Freezing solid
exercise: «The news of the discovery is still reverberating around the world.» What does reverberating mean here? | Sinking to the bottom | Being hidden away | Shrinking in size | *Spreading and having effects for a long time
exercise: Why does a hall with hard, bare walls make a sound reverberate? | The walls are made of water | *The sound bounces off the hard walls again and again | The sound stops at once | Soft curtains soak up the sound
test: «The thunder's reverberating boom shook the windows.» What does reverberating tell you about the boom? | It was far too small to hear | It came from under the sea | It was a sudden, quiet pop | *It kept echoing and filling the air
test: Which word is closest in meaning to reverberating? | *Echoing | Quiet | Gentle | Broken
exam: Ben says reverberating means very quiet, because it sounds soft. Is he right? | Yes, it means very quiet and soft | Yes, it means broken into pieces | No, it means the same as sleeping | *No, it means a loud sound echoing again and again
